$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The application and its background</h2>
<p>The application — I.A. 5080/2026 in a testamentary case of 2014 — sought permission for PW-6, a woman of about 71 years living in Uttarakhand with age-related ailments and a doctor’s advice of rest, to be cross-examined through video conferencing. Her three earlier trips to Delhi, on 5 November 2024, 10 February 2025 and 24 July 2025, had each ended without her cross-examination taking place, for reasons attributable to neither her nor her evidence: an adjournment for opposing counsel’s illness, the court being on leave, and a bereavement in the examining counsel’s family. The respondents did not dispute that she is a crucial witness or that her health difficulties are real.</p>

<h2>The transitional question: 2021 Rules or 2025 Rules?</h2>
<p>The legal interest of the judgment lies in its handling of the rulebook. The High Court of Delhi Rules for Video Conferencing for Courts, 2021 — whose Rules 8.12 and 8.15 permit recording evidence from a remote location where sickness, infirmity, or undue delay and expense make attendance difficult — were repealed by the Electronic Evidence and Video Conferencing Rules, 2025. The Court held that Rule 27(2)(a) of the 2025 Rules preserves the 2021 framework for proceedings pending when the new rules commenced, so the application fell to be decided under the 2021 Rules. It then went further, testing the request against Rules 13(8) and 13(9) of the 2025 Rules as well, and found it permissible under either, since no party had shown any impediment to the effective recording of the testimony.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>That belt-and-braces approach gives practitioners a working rule for the transition: in pending matters, cite the 2021 Rules through the savings clause, but be prepared to satisfy the 2025 standards too — a request sound under both is effectively unassailable.</p>
</div>

<h2>The safeguards ordered</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep">Scheduling by the Joint Registrar (Judicial), with the VC link tested in advance.</div>
<div class="fstep">Examination first at a designated VC facility in Uttarakhand; recording from the witness’s residence only if her health prevents travel to the facility.</div>
<div class="fstep">Identity verification, and confirmation on record that the witness testifies voluntarily and free of influence.</div>
<div class="fstep">Only essential persons present at her location, each disclosed to the Court; documents shown to her as the Rules and the Registrar direct.</div>
<div class="fstep">Deposition read over or explained and authenticated; audio or video recording and dissemination by anyone unauthorised strictly prohibited; expenses initially borne by the applicant.</div>
</div>

<h2>The balance the order strikes</h2>
<p>Two interests pull against each other whenever remote evidence is sought: the convenience and dignity of a witness for whom repeated court journeys are a hardship, and the opposing party’s right to a searching, effective cross-examination. The judgment protects both. The witness is spared a fourth journey to Delhi; the cross-examining party keeps every substantive right, with the Court recording expressly that the respondent’s right to test the testimony of PW-6 must remain fully protected and that the order concerns only the mode of recording.</p>
<p>For litigators, the case is a template. Where a witness is elderly, unwell or distant, an application supported by medical advice and a concrete history of frustrated attendance — as here — stands on strong ground, and the conditions imposed in this order are a ready checklist for drafting the prayer: venue hierarchy, identity verification, presence disclosure, document protocol, authentication and costs.</p>
